traceability.evidence:
1) Story linkage and implementation file:
- File docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md exists.
- Rule implementation file src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts exists and is annotated with:
  /**
   * Rule to validate @req annotation references refer to existing requirements in story files
   * @story docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md
   * @req REQ-DEEP-PARSE - Parse comments and extract story/requirement metadata
   * @req REQ-DEEP-MATCH - Match @req annotations to story file requirements
   * @req REQ-DEEP-CACHE - Cache requirement IDs per story file for efficient validation
   * @req REQ-DEEP-PATH - Validate and resolve story file paths safely
   */

2) Deep parsing and requirement existence validation (REQ-DEEP-PARSE, REQ-DEEP-MATCH, REQ-DEEP-FORMAT, REQ-DEEP-SECTION):
- loadAndCacheRequirements() in src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts:
  function loadAndCacheRequirements(opts: { resolvedStoryPath: string; reqCache: Map<string, Set>; }): Set {
    ...
    if (!reqCache.has(resolvedStoryPath)) {
      try {
        const content = fs.readFileSync(resolvedStoryPath, "utf8");
        const found = new Set();
        const regex = /REQ-[A-Z0-9-]+/g;
        let match: RegExpExecArray | null;
        while ((match = regex.exec(content)) !== null) {
          found.add(match[0]);
        }
        reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, found);
      } catch {
        reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, new Set());
      }
    }
    return reqCache.get(resolvedStoryPath)!;
  }
- checkRequirementExists() reports an error if the requirement is not found:
  function checkRequirementExists(opts: { comment: any; context: any; reqId: string; storyPath: string; reqSet: Set; }): void {
    ...
    if (!reqSet.has(reqId)) {
      context.report({
        node: comment as any,
        messageId: "reqMissing",
        data: { reqId, storyPath },
      });
    }
  }
- The regex /REQ-[A-Z0-9-]+/g matches requirement identifiers anywhere in the markdown content, including bullet lists and different sections, rather than relying on a rigid section structure. Example fixture:
  tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md:
  - REQ-BULLET-LIST - a requirement in bullet list format
  - REQ-ANOTHER - another requirement
- Multi-story fixtures show support for repeated IDs across files:
  tests/fixtures/story_multi_a.md:
  - REQ-SHARED-ID
  - REQ-ONLY-A
  tests/fixtures/story_multi_b.md:
  - REQ-SHARED-ID
  - REQ-ONLY-B

3) Path validation and safe resolution (part of REQ-DEEP-PATH and Error Handling):
- validateAndResolveStoryPath() in src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts:
  function validateAndResolveStoryPath(opts: { comment: any; context: any; storyPath: string; cwd: string; }): string | null {
    ...
    if (storyPath.includes("..") || path.isAbsolute(storyPath)) {
      context.report({ node: comment as any, messageId: "invalidPath", data: { storyPath } });
      return null;
    }
    const resolvedStoryPath = path.resolve(cwd, storyPath);
    if (!resolvedStoryPath.startsWith(cwd + path.sep) && resolvedStoryPath !== cwd) {
      context.report({ node: comment as any, messageId: "invalidPath", data: { storyPath } });
      return null;
    }
    return resolvedStoryPath;
  }
- This prevents traversal and absolute paths and reports a clear ESLint error instead of throwing.

4) Caching behavior (REQ-DEEP-CACHE, performance consideration):
- programListener(context) creates a shared Map<string, Set> reqCache per ESLint run:
  const reqCache = new Map<string, Set>();
- loadAndCacheRequirements() uses this map to avoid re-reading and re-parsing story files:
  if (!reqCache.has(resolvedStoryPath)) { ... fs.readFileSync ... reqCache.set(...) }
- All validations for a given story file reuse the same cached Set of requirement IDs, satisfying the caching requirement.

5) Integration with annotation parsing and existing rules (Integration criterion):
- Annotation handling functions in src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts:
  - extractStoryPath(comment): parses @story lines from JSDoc-style comments.
  - extractReqIdFromLine(line): extracts the requirement ID from @req lines.
  - validateReqLine(...) uses storyPath, resolves story, loads requirements, and calls checkRequirementExists.
  - parseImplementsLine(line) and validateImplementsLine(...) support @implements annotations with multiple requirement IDs and their own story paths.
  - handleAnnotationLine(...) routes @story, @req, and @implements lines in a unified way.
  - processCommentLines(...) and processAllComments(...) iterate all comments from context.getSourceCode().
  - programListener(context) returns a Program visitor that invokes processAllComments.
- This shows the deep validation rule is integrated into the same comment/annotation infrastructure as other traceability rules, not a separate system.
- src/index.ts includes the rule among plugin exports:
  const RULE_NAMES = [
    "require-story-annotation",
    "require-req-annotation",
    "require-branch-annotation",
    "valid-annotation-format",
